package CodeList
import Trigger

/*	CodeList

	An ordered list of `code` values that is run in one go, in the thread that runs it.

	Jass cannot keep a `code` value in an array or a class field, so a library that wants to call a
	set of registered callbacks has had to keep a trigger and evaluate it. That costs the engine's
	round trip on every dispatch: measured on the 3.0.0 client, about 4 us per listener, where a
	closure list costs about 0.12 us. The Lua backend lowers the three functions below to one table
	per list, an append, and a loop of calls, so a listener costs a call.

	The bodies of the functions are the Jass implementation, a trigger per list, and they are also
	what any compiler without the lowering compiles on both targets. They are correct in source, so
	nothing degrades silently: such a build only pays the trigger.

	The functions are recognised by the compiler through their annotation and their exact
	signatures, so they must keep these shapes.

	What a list promises, on both targets:
	- The code runs in the order it was added, once per run.
	- It runs like a trigger condition, in the current thread. It must not wait.
	- A callback that fails does not stop the ones after it.
	- Code added while the list runs may or may not run in that run.
*/

// Jass fallback state: the trigger behind each list. Lua does not touch it.
trigger array listTriggers
var listCount = 0

/** Creates an empty list. It lives as long as the map does. */
@compilerintrinsic public function codeListCreate() returns int
	listTriggers[listCount] = CreateTrigger()
	listCount++
	return listCount - 1

/** Adds code to the end of the list. */
@compilerintrinsic public function codeListAdd(int list, code c)
	listTriggers[list].addCondition(Condition(c))

/** Runs every code value of the list, in the order added. */
@compilerintrinsic public function codeListRun(int list)
	listTriggers[list].evaluate()

/** The intrinsics above, as a class. */
public class CodeList
	private int list

	construct()
		list = codeListCreate()

	/** Adds code to the end of the list. */
	function add(code c)
		codeListAdd(list, c)

	/** Runs every code value of the list, in the order added, in the current thread. It must not wait. */
	function run()
		codeListRun(list)
